  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Perdita bradleyi is a small, solitary bee species belonging to the Panurginae subfamily, known for its delicate structure and important role as a pollinator in New England ecosystems. These diminutive bees are part of the diverse Perdita genus, which comprises some of the smallest bee species in North America.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Tiny Size",
      "description": "Perdita bradleyi is one of the smallest bee species, typically measuring only 3-4 mm in length, making it difficult to spot with the naked eye.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Sparse Body Setae",
      "description": "This species has relatively sparse hair coverage compared to other bees, giving it a smoother appearance under magnification.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Metallic Coloration",
      "description": "The bee exhibits subtle metallic sheens on its head and thorax, with a generally dark body coloration.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Perdita bradleyi inhabits open, sandy or well-drained areas in New England where its preferred native flowering plants are abundant during its active season.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite their microscopic size, Perdita bees are solitary nesters that excavate individual burrows in sandy soil, with females constructing and provisioning their own brood cells without assistance from other bees.",
